[2026-03-04T21:24:53.476Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Pg5N3jZBj917M4bfLLeiDP","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay CLI Reference\n\n**Replay CLI** is a command-line tool for recording web applications, gathering performance data, and debugging issues. Here's the complete documentation:\n\n## Installation\n\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\n## Authentication\n\nGenerate an API key from https://app.replay.io settings and set it as the `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n## Key Commands\n\n| Command | Purpose |\n|---------|---------|\n| `record [url]` | Launch Replay Browser to record a session |\n| `upload [ids...]` | Upload recordings to the cloud |\n| `list` | Display all local recordings |\n| `remove [ids...]` | Delete recordings locally |\n| `login/logout` | Manage account access |\n| `info` | Show installed versions and browser info |\n| `upload-source-maps` | Upload source maps for debugging |\n\n## Common Workflow\n\n1. **Record a session:** `replayio record https://myapp.com`\n2. **Upload when ready:** `replayio upload <recording-id>`\n3. **Manage recordings:** Use `list` to view and `remove` to delete\n\n## MCP Server Integration\n\nFor debugging recorded applications within Claude, configure the Replay MCP server using the provided configuration with your `REPLAY_API_KEY`.\n\n**Note:** Use `-h` or `--help` flags with any command for detailed options."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ce70166-0386-48d1-8d55-34d30a2c7a49","uuid":"2d179446-bddc-472a-af19-146955bf5f22","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4854,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay CLI Reference\n\n**Replay CLI** is a command-line tool for recording web applications, gathering performance data, and debugging issues. Here's the complete documentation:\n\n## Installation\n\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\n## Authentication\n\nGenerate an API key from https://app.replay.io settings and set it as the `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n## Key Commands\n\n| Command | Purpose |\n|---------|---------|\n| `record [url]` | Launch Replay Browser to record a session |\n| `upload [ids...]` | Upload recordings to the cloud |\n| `list` | Display all local recordings |\n| `remove [ids...]` | Delete recordings locally |\n| `login/logout` | Manage account access |\n| `info` | Show installed versions and browser info |\n| `upload-source-maps` | Upload source maps for debugging |\n\n## Common Workflow\n\n1. **Record a session:** `replayio record https://myapp.com`\n2. **Upload when ready:** `replayio upload <recording-id>`\n3. **Manage recordings:** Use `list` to view and `remove` to delete\n\n## MCP Server Integration\n\nFor debugging recorded applications within Claude, configure the Replay MCP server using the provided configuration with your `REPLAY_API_KEY`.\n\n**Note:** Use `-h` or `--help` flags with any command for detailed options.","durationMs":3570,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md"}}
[2026-03-04T21:24:53.980Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Czcm7RCnHriW5CLXYVePsQ","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay MCP Documentation Summary\n\n**Replay MCP** is a tool for inspecting Replay.io recordings, which capture complete browser execution data. As stated in the overview, you can \"explore and investigate the app's behavior as if you are a detective with a time machine.\"\n\n## Key Investigation Approach\n\nThe documentation recommends a structured methodology:\n1. Frame your investigation as a specific question\n2. Use exploratory tools to identify relevant events\n3. Apply explanatory tools to understand causation\n4. Form and validate hypotheses with gathered evidence\n5. Support conclusions with data\n\n## Tool Categories\n\n**Exploratory Tools** discover what happened:\n- Error tracking (uncaught exceptions, React errors)\n- Timeline analysis (console messages, network requests, React renders, storage access)\n- Source code inspection and searching\n\n**Explanatory Tools** clarify why events occurred:\n- Stack traces and control dependencies\n- Point descriptions with variable values\n- DOM element and React component inspection\n- Expression evaluation and logpoints\n\n## Core Concepts\n\nA \"point\" represents each discrete execution moment, while an \"element\" denotes a persistent DOM reference independent of time. The recording functions as \"a gigantic database containing everything that happened while the app executed.\"\n\n## Practical Recommendations\n\nThe documentation emphasizes using logpoints to track expression values across execution, examining whether relevant code executed at all, and analyzing timing dependencies when investigating delayed events."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ce70166-0386-48d1-8d55-34d30a2c7a49","uuid":"0cb1c62b-6d7c-4b92-9dbc-48ecb9c4b19b","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4555,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay MCP Documentation Summary\n\n**Replay MCP** is a tool for inspecting Replay.io recordings, which capture complete browser execution data. As stated in the overview, you can \"explore and investigate the app's behavior as if you are a detective with a time machine.\"\n\n## Key Investigation Approach\n\nThe documentation recommends a structured methodology:\n1. Frame your investigation as a specific question\n2. Use exploratory tools to identify relevant events\n3. Apply explanatory tools to understand causation\n4. Form and validate hypotheses with gathered evidence\n5. Support conclusions with data\n\n## Tool Categories\n\n**Exploratory Tools** discover what happened:\n- Error tracking (uncaught exceptions, React errors)\n- Timeline analysis (console messages, network requests, React renders, storage access)\n- Source code inspection and searching\n\n**Explanatory Tools** clarify why events occurred:\n- Stack traces and control dependencies\n- Point descriptions with variable values\n- DOM element and React component inspection\n- Expression evaluation and logpoints\n\n## Core Concepts\n\nA \"point\" represents each discrete execution moment, while an \"element\" denotes a persistent DOM reference independent of time. The recording functions as \"a gigantic database containing everything that happened while the app executed.\"\n\n## Practical Recommendations\n\nThe documentation emphasizes using logpoints to track expression values across execution, examining whether relevant code executed at all, and analyzing timing dependencies when investigating delayed events.","durationMs":3566,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md"}}
